{"id": "573334094776f41900660786", "title": "Financial_crisis_of_2007%E2%80%9308", "context": "Bernanke explained that between 1996 and 2004, the U.S. current account deficit increased by $650 billion, from 1.5% to 5.8% of GDP. Financing these deficits required the country to borrow large sums from abroad, much of it from countries running trade surpluses. These were mainly the emerging economies in Asia and oil-exporting nations. The balance of payments identity requires that a country (such as the U.S.) running a current account deficit also have a capital account (investment) surplus of the same amount. Hence large and growing amounts of foreign funds (capital) flowed into the U.S. to finance its imports.", "question": "Per Bernanke, how much did the U.S. current account deficit increase between 1996 and 2004?", "answers": {"text": ["$650 billion"], "answer_start": [93]}}
