{"id": "56df0244c65bf219000b3ecc", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "When not celebrating Mass but still serving a liturgical function, such as the semiannual Urbi et Orbi papal blessing, some Papal Masses and some events at Ecumenical Councils, cardinal deacons can be recognized by the dalmatics they would don with the simple white mitre (so called mitra simplex).", "question": "What color is the hat?", "answers": {"text": ["white"], "answer_start": [260]}}
{"id": "56df02b23277331400b4d8a7", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "As of 2005, there were over 50 churches recognized as cardinalatial deaconries, though there were only 30 cardinals of the order of deacons. Cardinal deacons have long enjoyed the right to \"opt for the order of cardinal priests\" (optazione) after they have been cardinal deacons for 10 years. They may on such elevation take a vacant \"title\" (a church allotted to a cardinal priest as the church in Rome with which he is associated) or their diaconal church may be temporarily elevated to a cardinal priest's \"title\" for that occasion. When elevated to cardinal priests, they take their precedence according to the day they were first made cardinal deacons (thus ranking above cardinal priests who were elevated to the college after them, regardless of order).", "question": "How many cardinal deacons were there in 2005?", "answers": {"text": ["30"], "answer_start": [103]}}
{"id": "56df02b23277331400b4d8a8", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "As of 2005, there were over 50 churches recognized as cardinalatial deaconries, though there were only 30 cardinals of the order of deacons. Cardinal deacons have long enjoyed the right to \"opt for the order of cardinal priests\" (optazione) after they have been cardinal deacons for 10 years. They may on such elevation take a vacant \"title\" (a church allotted to a cardinal priest as the church in Rome with which he is associated) or their diaconal church may be temporarily elevated to a cardinal priest's \"title\" for that occasion. When elevated to cardinal priests, they take their precedence according to the day they were first made cardinal deacons (thus ranking above cardinal priests who were elevated to the college after them, regardless of order).", "question": "How many years before a cardinal deacon can choose the order of the cardinal priests?", "answers": {"text": ["10 years"], "answer_start": [283]}}
{"id": "56df03d9c65bf219000b3ecf", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "Cardinals elevated to the diaconal order are mainly officials of the Roman Curia holding various posts in the church administration. Their number and influence has varied through the years. While historically predominantly Italian the group has become much more internationally diverse in later years. While in 1939 about half were Italian by 1994 the number was reduced to one third. Their influence in the election of the Pope has been considered important, they are better informed and connected than the dislocated cardinals but their level of unity has been varied. Under the 1587 decree of Pope Sixtus V, which fixed the maximum size of the College of Cardinals, there were 14 cardinal deacons. Later the number increased. As late as 1939 almost half of the cardinals were members of the curia. Pius XII reduced this percentage to 24 percent. John XXIII brought it back up to 37 percent but Paul VI brought it down to 27 percent where John Paul II has maintained this ratio.", "question": "In 1939, how many Cardinals were Italian?", "answers": {"text": ["about half"], "answer_start": [316]}}
{"id": "56df03d9c65bf219000b3ed0", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "Cardinals elevated to the diaconal order are mainly officials of the Roman Curia holding various posts in the church administration. Their number and influence has varied through the years. While historically predominantly Italian the group has become much more internationally diverse in later years. While in 1939 about half were Italian by 1994 the number was reduced to one third. Their influence in the election of the Pope has been considered important, they are better informed and connected than the dislocated cardinals but their level of unity has been varied. Under the 1587 decree of Pope Sixtus V, which fixed the maximum size of the College of Cardinals, there were 14 cardinal deacons. Later the number increased. As late as 1939 almost half of the cardinals were members of the curia. Pius XII reduced this percentage to 24 percent. John XXIII brought it back up to 37 percent but Paul VI brought it down to 27 percent where John Paul II has maintained this ratio.", "question": "In 1994, how many Cardinals were Italian?", "answers": {"text": ["third"], "answer_start": [378]}}
{"id": "56df04563277331400b4d8b1", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "Cardinal deacons derive originally from the seven deacons in the Papal Household and the seven deacons who supervised the Church's works in the districts of Rome during the early Middle Ages, when church administration was effectively the government of Rome and provided all social services. Cardinal deacons are given title to one of these deaconries.", "question": "What is the name of one of the deaconries in which Cardinals come from?", "answers": {"text": ["seven deacons in the Papal Household"], "answer_start": [44]}}
{"id": "56df04ab3277331400b4d8b5", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "The cardinal deacons are the lowest-ranking cardinals. Cardinals elevated to the diaconal order are either officials of the Roman Curia or priests elevated after their 80th birthday. Bishops with diocesan responsibilities, however, are created cardinal priests.", "question": "Which cardinals are ranked lowest?", "answers": {"text": ["cardinal deacons"], "answer_start": [4]}}
{"id": "56df05323277331400b4d8c1", "title": "Cardinal_(Catholicism)", "context": "The cardinal who is the longest-serving member of the order of cardinal priests is titled cardinal protopriest. He had certain ceremonial duties in the conclave that have effectively ceased because he would generally have already reached age 80, at which cardinals are barred from the conclave. The current cardinal protopriest is Paulo Evaristo Arns of Brazil.", "question": "Who can become the cardinal protopriest?", "answers": {"text": ["The cardinal who is the longest-serving member of the order of cardinal priests"], "answer_start": [0]}}
